Abstract
In this work, biodegradable cellulose-based biosorbents (MOFs/OCBs) with dual function of dye removal and microbial inactivation were fabricated by in situ anchoring metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) on the TEMPO oxidized corncobs (OCBs). Results showed that delignification and oxidation can develop the OCBs with more cellulose content, carboxyl groups and specific surface area, thus facilitating the deposition of MOFs. The porous and carbohydrate-rich OCBs can serve as supports and stabilizers for MOFs, allowing for enhanced stability and recyclability of MOFs powders. The MOFs, namely HKUST-1 and ZIF-8, can endow the OCBs multiple functions of good adsorption capacity to methyl orange (from $8\%$ of OCBs to $55\%$ of HKUST-1/OCBs and $84\%$ of ZIF-8/OCBs) and excellent antibacterial activity (from 0 of OCBs to $90.2\%$ of HKUST-1/OCBs and $44.8\%$ of
ZIF-8/OCBs). Such a concept may offer a new pathway for preparing economical and efficient biosorbents for environmental remedy purposes.
Keywords: Biosorbent; cellulose-rich corncob; MOFs; dye removal; microbial inactivation
## 1. Introduction
The efficient treatment of wastewater from different sectors has attracted extensive attention worldwide in recent years (Bo et al., 2018; Gągol, Przyjazny, & Boczkaj, 2018; Li, Mu, & Yang, 2019; Ren et al., 2018). The effluents from some industrial sectors, such as pulp and paper, textile, printing and dyeing, dairy effluents contain high synthetic dyes, microbial contaminants, chemical oxygen demand, suspended solids and heavy metals, etc. (Shamsnejati, Chaibakhsh, Pendashteh, & Hayeripour, 2015; Zhang, Wang, Gong, & Shen, 2017). In particular, most of synthetic dyes in the effluents involved in nondegradable aromatic structure are considered to be toxic and even carcinogenic, to human health and ecological security, and the presence of microbial contaminations in effluents can also give rise to various serious public health threats (Chaibakhsh, Ahmadi, & Zanjanchi, 2014; Gerba, Betancourt, Kitajima, & Rock, 2018).
Many techniques have been employed for effluent treatment such as adsorption, coagulation, biological treatment, membrane filtration and advanced oxidation using Fenton and electrocatalysis etc. (Guo, Gao, & Fatehi, 2018; Li et al., 2019; Ren et al., 2018). Among them, adsorption has been regarded to be green and environmentally friendly one due to high efficiency, easy operation and wide adaptability (Jiang et al., 2019; Ma et al., 2019). A number of adsorbents have been developed for above purpose, but most of them may suffer from high cost and inconvenient separation, which impede the wide applications of these adsorbents (Li et al., 2019; Rangabhashiyam, Jayabalan, Rajkumar, & Balasubramanian, 2019). Therefore, it is of high demand to develop more economic, efficient and practical adsorbents for the purpose above.
Agricultural wastes would be attractive building blocks for the value-added biosorbent because of their abundance, low cost, special structures and excellent properties (Hu et al., 2018; Jain, Balasubramanian, & Srinivasan, 2016). A large number of corncobs are generated as agricultural waste during the processing of corns and most of them are usually discarded or burnt, causing serious environmental pollution or no benefit (Hu et al., 2018). In the literature, corncobs have been investigated as precursor for the preparation of corncob derivatives and activated carbon for various applications including water purification, adsorbent, biocatalysts and hydrogen storage (Hu et al., 2018; Ma et al., 2015; Zhang et al., 2017). Considering this, corncobs may be converted as value-added biosorbents for the removal of toxic pollutants and inhibition of microbial growth from wastewaters.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), an emerging class of crystalline materials consisting of metal nodes and organic ligands, have captured widespread interest due to their high porosity and surface area, flexible tunability, and well-defined architecture (Chen, Luque, & Li, 2017; Duan et al., 2018). These favorable features make MOFs very promising in the applications of water purification, gas adsorption and storage, heterogeneous catalysis and antimicrobial systems, etc. (Chen et al., 2017; Jiang et al., 2019; Wang, Qian, & An, 2015). However, the main problem of using MOFs as adsorbents is too difficult to recovery them from residual solution after use because of their nano or micro size (Feng et al., 2016; Lei et al., 2019; Ren et al., 2018).
Herein, in this work, we propose a novel concept of using corncobs as the carriers/supports for MOFs anchoring to prepare an economic and efficient biosorbents. The hypothesis is that the carbohydrate-rich corncobs with high porosity, specific surface areas and functional groups can facilitate the crystallization and immobilization of copper- and zinc-based MOFs, thus improving the stability and recyclability of the absorbents, and those anchored porous MOFs can enable the adsorption capacity and antibacterial activity of the as-prepared biosorbents.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1 Materials
Copper acetate monohydrate $(\mathbf{Cu(OAC)}_{2}.\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O})$ , trimesic acid $\mathrm{(H_{3}B T C)}$ , zinc nitrate hexahydrate $(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ and 2-methylimidazole were purchased from Macklin Reagent Co., Ltd., China. 2,2,6,6-Tetramethylpiperidine-1-oxyl (TEMPO), sodium bromide, sodium chlorite, acetic acid, sodium hypochlorite and methyl orange (MO) were obtained from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd., China. All chemicals and reagents used were analytical grade, without further purification.
## 2.2 Delignification and TEMPO oxidation of corncobs
The delignification of corncobs was conducted based on the modified sodium chlorite method. $\boldsymbol{10}\mathrm{\:g}$ of corncobs (oven dried weight) were put into $700~\mathrm{mL}$ mixture solution containing $\mathrm{~7~g~}$ sodium chlorite and $2.3~\mathrm{mL}$ acetic acid at $80~^{\mathrm{{o}}}\mathrm{{C}}$ for 3h. The delignified corncobs (DCBs) were rinsed with distilled water to neutral $\mathfrak{p H}$ . The lignin content of corncobs decreased from $14.2~\%$ to $3.7~\%$ as result of sodium chlorite delignification.
The TEMPO oxidation of the delignified corncobs was performed according to the modified procedure reported in the literature (Valencia & Abdelhamid, 2019) and the details are carried out as follows: $\boldsymbol{10}\mathrm{\:g}$ of DCBs were poured into $400~\mathrm{{mL}}$ mixture solution that contains $\boldsymbol{0.1\mathrm{\textrm{g}}}$ TEMPO, 1g NaBr and $30~\mathrm{mL}$ of NaClO solution was slowly dropped into the beaker with continuous stirring and keep reaction for 4h, where the $\mathrm{\pH}$ in the oxidation system was maintained about 10. The oxidized corncobs (OCBs) were washed to neutral $\mathrm{pH}$ with deionized water, then collected and kept in a sealed bag. All the wet samples were stored in a sealed plastic bag at $4^{\mathrm{{\circ}}}\mathrm{C}$ .
## 2.3 Preparation of copper- and zinc- based MOFs and MOFs/ OCBs
The synthesis process of the Cu- and Zn- based MOFs (HKUST-1 and ZIF-8) was performed according to the modified procedure reported in the literature (Duan et al., 2018; Su et al., 2018; Valencia & Abdelhamid, 2019). The details are given as follows:
For the HKUST-1 sample, copper acetate monohydrate $\mathrm{261~mg}$ ) was dissolved in a $30~\mathrm{mL}$ of mixed solvent consisting of DMF, ethanol, water (volume ratio of 1:1:1, defined as Solvent I), and the solution was defined as Solution A; trimesic acid $(152\mathrm{mg})$ was also dissolved in $30~\mathrm{mL}$ of Solvent I, which was denoted as Solution B. After their complete dissolution, $30\mathrm{mL}$ of Solution $\textbf{B}$ (trimesic acid solution) were dropwise added to the Solution A (copper acetate solution) and vigorously stirred (at $300\mathrm{rpm},$ at room temperature. After that, the mixture was under ultrasonic dispersion $(3200~\mathrm{w})$ for $1~\mathrm{min}$ , and it was stirred with a magnetic stirrer at a slow stirring speed (200rpm), allowing the reaction for 1h. The resultant MOF sample was separated from the solution by centrifugation at $4000~\mathrm{rpm}$ and washed with solvent I. The resultant HKUST-1 sample (in blue color) was dried overnight at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$
For the preparation of ZIF-8 sample, the synthesis procedure was similar to that of HKUST-1 except that zinc nitrate hexahydrate ( $\mathrm{390~mg)}$ and 2-methylimidazole $\left(860~\mathrm{mg}\right)$ were dissolved in two set of $30~\mathrm{mL}$ of methanol, respectively (defined as Solution C and Solution D). The resultant ZIF-8 sample (in white color) was dried overnight at $80^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
For the HKUST-1/ OCBs, $3\mathrm{~g~}$ OCBs substrates were immersed in $45~\mathrm{mL}$ of solution A containing $670~\mathrm{mg}$ of copper acetate monohydrate for $2\mathrm{~h~}$ . After that, the OCBs were taken out and washed with Solvent I for $5~\mathrm{min}$ and then immersed in Solution B containing $470~\mathrm{mg}$ of trimesic acid for $2\mathrm{~h~}$ before rinsing with Solvent I. The obtained HKUST-1/ OCBs sample was thoroughly washed using Solvent I, and dried in a vacuum oven at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $5\mathrm{h}$ to obtain the blue product.
For the preparation of ZIF-8/ OCBs, the same procedure was followed except that $3\mathrm{~g~OCBs~}$ substrates were immersed in $34~\mathrm{mL}$ of Solution C containing $1000~\mathrm{{mg}}$ of zinc nitrate hexahydrate, and subsequently to $34\mathrm{mL}$ of solution D containing 2200 mg of 2-methylimidazole. The final products were washed with methanol, and dried in a vacuum oven at $80~^{\mathrm{{o}}}\mathrm{{C}}$ for $5\mathrm{h}$ to obtain the white product.
## 2.4 Dye adsorption measurement and adsorption kinetics
To study the adsorption capacity of corncob-based biosorbents, methyl orange (MO), a typical anionic organic dye, was used. The MO adsorption was conducted according to the modified method reported in the literature (Zhang et al., 2019), and the $\mathrm{\pH}$ and temperature was fixed at 5 and $25^{\mathrm{{\circ}}}\mathrm{C}$ , respectively. First, $_{\textrm{2g}}$ of the corncobs-based samples and $250~\mathrm{mg}$ of the MOFs samples were added into $50~\mathrm{mL}$ of dye with different initial dye concentration (from 10 ppm to $90~\mathrm{ppm}$ , and $50\ \mathrm{ppm}$ of initial concentration was the optimized one). These samples were shaken on a bath at 150 rpm for different times (30, 60, 120, 240 and $360\ \mathrm{\min};$ , and aliquots of approximately $3\mathrm{\mL}$ were taken using a syringe, filtered on a $0.45~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ filter at different time intervals during the adsorption process.
The concentration of dyes was measured using a UV-vis spectroscope (UV5, Metter Toledo, Switzerland) at $463\ \mathrm{nm}$ , and calculated according to an established calibration. The adsorption capacity of MO on the biosorbents is calculated based on the Eq. (1):
$$
Q_{t}=V(C_{0}-C_{t})/m
$$
where $V$ is the solution volume, $C_{\cal{O}}$ is the initial MO concentration, $C_{t}$ is the MO concentration in the solution at a given time $(t)$ , and $m$ is the adsorbent mass.
The adsorption kinetics were also calculated and determined according to pseudo-first-order (Eq. 2) and pseudo-second-order models (Eq. 3), respectively, as described below:
$$
\log(Q_{e}-Q_{t})=\log(Q_{e})-\frac{k_{1}t}{2.303}
$$
$$
\frac{t}{\boldsymbol{Q}_{t}}=\frac{1}{(k_{2}\boldsymbol{Q}_{e}^{2})}+\frac{t}{\boldsymbol{Q}_{e}}
$$
where $k_{I}$ is the pseudo-first-order rate constant $(\mathrm{mg~g^{-1}~\ m i n^{-1}})$ , $k_{2}$ is the pseudo-second-order rate constant $(\mathbf{mg\textnormal{g}^{-1}\ m i n}^{-1})$ , $Q_{e}$ is the sorption capacity at equilibrium, $Q_{t}$ is the sorption at the time of $t$ .
## 2.5 Recyclability measurement
A set of recyclability tests were carried out in order to verify whether the corncob and MOFs composite materials can be reused for dye capture. After the completion of first adsorption, the used HKUST-1/ OCBs and ZIF-8/ OCBs were
thoroughly washed with ethanol or methanol to remove the adsorbed dye molecules.   
The adsorbents were dried overnight at room temperature prior to the next adsorption.   
This process is recycled four times.
## 2.6 Antimicrobial activity test
To investigate the antibacterial properties of HKUST-1/ OCBs and ZIF-8/ OCBs, a shaking flask method was performed and gram-negative bacteria Escherichia coli (E. coli) was selected as the model bacterium. To maintain the same amounts of Cu and Zn ions in the samples, $43.17~\mathrm{mg}$ of HKUST-1, $0.5\ \mathrm{g}$ of HKUST-1/ OCBs, 48.14 mg ZIF-8 and $_\textrm{1g}$ of ZIF-8/ OCBs were added into $5~\mathrm{mL}$ of $0.01\mathrm{~M~}$ phosphate buffered saline (PBS) solutions. The samples in Erlenmeyer flasks were shaken at $150\mathrm{rpm}$ for 30 minutes on a water bath shaker $(37^{\mathrm{{o}}}\mathrm{{C}).0.1\mathrm{{mL}}}$ of each was inoculated onto an agar dish, and then the plate was incubated at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . The number of the colonies was counted and the growth inhibition of bacteria was calculated based on Eq. (4):
Growthinhibition of bacteria (GIB) $)(\%)=(\mathbf{A}-\mathbf{B})/\mathbf{A}\times100\%$
Where A and B are the number of the colonies detected from the control and the treated samples, respectively. The number of colonies was calculated by using the image analysis technique, with ImageJ software. Three repetitions were conducted to get an average for each sample.
## 2.4 Analyses
The loadings of HKUST-1 and ZIF-8 on OCBs were calculated based on the amounts of copper and zinc present in the samples. Determination of metal ions was carried out on an inductively coupled plasma atomic emission spectrometer (ICP-AES, IRIS Intrepid II, Thermo Fisher, USA) (Duan et al., 2019). Prior to ICP-AES analysis, $_{\textrm{0.01~g}}$ of the sample was digested using $6~\mathrm{mL}$ of $\mathrm{HNO}_{3}$ and $2{\mathrm{~mL~}}$ of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ in a microwave system (Anton Paar). This solution was allowed to cool completely and then transferred to a $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ volumetric flask. The measurements were taken in triplicates and an average value was reported.
The Scanning Electron Microscope (SEM) characterizations of corncobs, MOFs and MOFs/ OCBs biosorbents samples were carried out on a VEGA 3S-4800 SEM (Hitachi, Tokyo, Japan).
The X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns were performed using a D8 Advance X-ray diffractometer (Bruker, Germany) with a scan range of 2θ recorded from $5^{\mathrm{{o}}}$ to $60^{\mathrm{o}}$ at a scan rate of $6^{\mathrm{{o}}}/\mathrm{min}$ .
Fourier Transform Infrared spectroscopy (FT-IR, TENSOR II, Bruker Optics Corporation; Germany) was carried out to qualitatively evaluate the functional groups present in sample surface. The spectra were collected in the range of $350{\mathrm{-}}4000{\mathrm{cm}}^{-1}$ .
The gas adsorption isotherms of the corncobs, MOFs and MOFs/ OCBs were measured using Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) method at Micromeritics Instrument Corporation, Gemini VII Version 2.00 using ${\bf N}_{2}$ as adsorption source at $77~\mathrm{K}$ . All samples were degassed by ${\bf N}_{2}$ blowing at $105^{\mathrm{{\circ}}}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{\textrm{1h}}$ to remove the humidity and volatile composites before testing. The specific surface area of samples was calculated by ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms at relative vapor pressures of $0.05{-}0.3\mathrm{MPa}$ .
## 3. Results and discussion
The concept of converting corncobs to value-added biosorbents for wastewater treatment is shown in Fig. 1. Herein, the pristine corncobs (CBs) were subjected to delignification to obtain the delignified corncobs (DCBs), then TEMPO-oxidation to obtain the oxidized corncobs (OCBs) so that higher carbohydrates and carboxyl group contents in the OCBs matrix are obtained, both of which are favorable to form coordination with copper and zinc ions, facilitating the subsequent anchoring/growing of MOFs crystals during the in-situ synthesis processes; subsequently, HKUST-1/ OCBs and ZIF-8/ OCBs were prepared, and were used as biosorbents for dyes removal and microbial inactivation from wastewater samples. These Cu- and Znbased MOFs supported OCBs, with high porosity and surface area, and high tunability can have good adsorption capacity for dyes and excellent bacterial inactivation. In addition, due to the "supporting/protecting" role of corncobs, the stability and recyclability of the HKUST-1/ OCBs and ZIF-8/ OCBs in the wastewater treatment process would be facilitated. Thus, the concept illustrated in Fig. 1 may provide an economic, efficient process for dye removal and microbials inactivity in wastewater.
The changes of carboxyl content in the TEMPO oxidized corncobs (OCBs) and the corresponding loading of MOFs are shown in Fig. 2. The carboxyl groups were rapidly formed as the TEMPO oxidation continued, and its content increased from $8.66~\mathrm{mmol/l00g}$ of the pristine corncobs to $62.82\ \mathrm{mmol/100g}$ after a 4h treatment, and further to $67.13\mathrm{\mmol/100g}$ after a 6h oxidation. The increased carboxyl group content favors the formation of MOF on the OCB surfaces and the loadings of both HKUST-1s and ZIF-8s on those corncobs were more with the increased carboxyl contents, which is mainly due to the availability of more carboxyl groups. As shown in Fig. 2b and 2c, the reaction mechanism for immobilization of MOFs on OCBs mainly refers to the electrostatic interaction and coordination between -COOH in modified OCBs and metal ions in MOFs.
In the literature, it was reported that carboxymethylation can impart cellulose fibers with more reaction sites (carboxyl groups) for the MOFs crystal anchoring/ growing (Duan et al., 2018) and the presence of a large number of carboxyl groups offering abundant binding sites for nucleation, growth and adhesion of MOF crystals (Valencia & Abdelhamid, 2019). Based on the above results, the condition of TEMPO oxidation of corncob for 4h was used in the subsequent studies.
## 3.3 SEM analyses
Fig. 3 shows the SEM images of corncobs under various conditions: pristine corncobs (CBs, Fig. 3a), delignified corncobs (DCBs, Fig. 3b), TEMPO-oxidized corncobs (OCBs, Fig. 3c), HKUST-1/ OCBs (Fig. 3e) and ZIF-8/ OCBs (Fig. 3f). The untreated CBs exhibited honeycomb-like morphology with porous surfaces in a range of $50\mathrm{-}100~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ (Fig. 3a). The inherent porous structure of corncob will enable it as potential carriers for absorbents. In comparison to the CBs, the treated corncobs after delignification and oxidation had rough surfaces, with more cavities (Fig. 3b and 3c), which is attributed to the partial removal of lignin (insets in Fig. 3a and 3b) and oxidation of carbohydrates during these treatments. Many of the hydroxyl groups on the corncobs were oxidized to carboxyl groups as a result of the TEMPO oxidation, providing many needed active sites for the growth of MOFs. As shown in Fig. 3e and Fig. 3f, the OCBs were loaded by the octahedral HKUST-1s ( $400\mathrm{nm}$ to a few microns) and dodecahedral ZIF-8s ( $200~\mathrm{nm}$ to $500~\mathrm{{nm}}$ ) (insets in Fig. 3e and 3f), which are consistent with the result that carboxylates have strong affinities for metal cations, forming metal-carboxylate complexes (Matsumoto & Kitaoka, 2016). However, compared to the exterior OCBs, the deposition of MOFs on the internal OCBs rapidly decreased due to the increased steric hindrance against the penetration of metal ions and organic ligands and decreased active sites of oxidized carboxyl groups inside the corncobs (seen in digital photos and SEM images in Fig. 1S). Therefore, reducing the particle size of corncobs can indeed increase the -COOH content and the deposition of MOFs on these corncobs, thus enhancing the follow-up adsorption and antibacterial effects of the composites, which will be discussed in our next work.
## 3.4 XRD analyses
The XRD patterns of OCBs, HKUST-1/ OCBs and ZIF-8/ OCBs are shown in Fig. 4. The OCBs sample had characteristic cellulose I structure, with distinct allomorph and crystalline peaks (Ma et al., 2014). The peaks at $2\theta=6.7^{\circ}$ , $9.5^{\circ}$ , $11.7^{\circ}$ , $13.3^{\circ}$ , $16.6^{\circ}$ , $18.9^{\circ}$ , $25.8^{\circ}$ and $29.2^{\circ}$ can be attributed to HKUST-1 (Duan et al., 2018) while the peaks at $2\uptheta=7.3^{\circ}$ , $10.4^{\circ}$ , $12.7^{\circ}$ , $14.7^{\circ}$ , $16.4^{\circ}$ and $18.0^{\circ}$ can be assigned to ZIF-8 nanocrystals (Su et al., 2018), supporting the conclusion that HKUST-1 (Fig. 4(a-iii) and ZIF-8 (Fig. 4(b-iii)) crystals were synthesized. For HKUST-1/ OCBs (Fig. 4(a-i)), and ZIF-8/ OCBs (Fig. 4(b-i)) samples, the characteristic peaks of HKUST-1 and ZIF-8 were evident, i.e., $2\theta{=}6.7^{\circ}$ , $9.5^{\circ}$ , $11.7^{\circ}$ , $18.9^{\circ}$ , $25.8^{\circ}$ and $2\theta{=}7.3^{\circ}$ , $10.4^{\circ}$ , $12.7^{\circ}$ , $18.0^{\circ}$ , respectively, confirming that the HKUST-1/ OCBs and ZIF-8/ OCBs samples were successfully prepared.
## 3.5 FT-IR analysis
The chemical structure of CBs, DCBs, OCBs, HKUST-1, HKUST-1/ OCBs, ZIF-8 and ZIF-8/ OCBs were analyzed using FT-IR spectra (Fig. 5). As shown in Fig. 5a. The broad peak at $3200{-}3600~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ represents the -OH stretching vibration, while the peak at $1505\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ corresponds to the aromatic ring absorption band of lignin (Li et al., 2017). Therefore, the absence of the peak at $1505~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ in DCBs was consistent with the delignification of CBs. For the OCBs, the strong peak at $1605~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ , can be attributed to the formation of $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C=O}$ stretching in -COOH groups as a result of TEMPO oxidation (Huang et al., 2019). In addition, Those peaks at 1648, 1448, 730 and 1375 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are due to the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ , C-O, Cu-O stretching and O-H bending vibration respectively in HKUST-1 structure (Fig. 5b) (Duan et al., 2018), while those peaks at 1584 and $421~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ , can be attributed to the stretching $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C}=\mathbf{N}$ and $Z\mathrm{n-N}$ in the ZIF-8 structure (Abbasi et al., 2016; He et al., 2014). For MOFs/ OCBs, the peaks at 1448, 1375 and $730\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (HKUST-1), and at 3315, 2929, 1584 and $421\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (ZIF-8) agreed with those spectra in HKUST-1 (Fig. 5b) and ZIF-8 (Fig. 5c), showing the presence of HKUST-1 and ZIF-8 in MOFs/ OCBs. Moreover, the blue-shifted $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C=O}$ peak on HKUST-1/ OCBs (from 1605 to $1646~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ ) and ZIF-8/ OCBs (from 1605 to $1624\mathrm{cm}^{-1}.$ ) can be attributed to the interactions of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ or $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ in MOFs with -COOH in OCBs (Gao et al., 2019). Therefore, the FT-IR results are consistent with the results of SEM and XRD, further confirming the formation of MOFs in the structure of MOFs/ OCBs.
## 3.6 Surface area analysis
The adsorption results of OCBs, MOFs and MOF/ OCBs samples towards methyl orange (MO) are presented in Fig. 6. The adsorption capacity gradually increased with time and nearly reached equilibrium in $60~\mathrm{min}$ . The adsorption capacity (or dye removal efficiency) of HKUST-1/ OCBs and ZIF-8/ OCBs samples were much higher in comparison with those of OCBs. For example, the MO removal efficiency was about $8\%$ for OCBs (from original 50 ppm to $45.7\ \mathrm{ppm}$ ), while it was $55\%$ (from $50\ \mathrm{ppm}$ to $22.5\mathrm{\ppm})$ and $84\%$ (from 50 ppm to $8.1\ \mathrm{\ppm},$ for the HKUST-1/ OCBs and ZIF-8/ OCBs, respectively, at the equilibrium. The enhanced adsorption capacity can be attributed to the immobilized MOFs. The adsorption capacity $(Q_{t})$ results of MOFs themselves calculated by formula (2) were $7.00~\mathrm{mg/g}$ for HKUST-1 sample and $21.74~\mathrm{mg/g}$ for ZIF-8 sample, which are consistent with those reported in the literature (Feng et al., 2016).
The higher adsorption rate and capacity of ZIF-8/ OCBs than that of HKUST-1/ OCBs, may be due to the higher SSA ( $1288.55\ \mathrm{m}^{2}/\$ g vs. $752.58\ \mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{~g}\rangle$ and higher positive charge density ( $23.8~\mathrm{mV}$ vs. $2.5~\mathrm{mV}$ ) of ZIF-8 (Table 1S), thus resulting in more adsorption sites and stronger electrostatic interactions with the negatively charged MO dyes. Park & Oh (2017) also reported that a flexible MOF-based paper with positive charge can effectively capture the negatively charged organic dyes due to the enhanced electrostatic interactions. For the HKUST-1 and HKUST-1/ OCBs, their adsorption capacities gradually decreased after $100\ \mathrm{min}$ which was mainly related to the water instability of HKUST-1 crystal (Rubin et al., 2017).
## 3.8 Adsorption kinetics
The pseudo first-order-model and the pseudo second-order-model are widely used in the analyses of adsorption process and kinetic. The calculated kinetic parameters of the MOFs/ OCBs samples are given in Fig. 7 and Table 2. It can be found that the time-dependence of the adsorption was well-fitted to a pseudo second-order kinetic model with rate constant $\mathbf{k}_{2}$ . Similarly, Table 2 shows that the second-order kinetics ${\tt R}^{2}$ of MOFs/OCBs adsorbs MO is higher than the first-order kinetics ${\bf R}^{2}$ . Lei et al. (2019) also found that the second-order kinetic is more reasonable for revealing the adsorption kinetics of UiO- $66@$ cellulose and UiO-66-NH2 $@$ cellulose aerogels towards heavy metal ions $({\mathrm{Pb}}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ ) in water.
## 3.8 Recyclability of biosorbents
The recycling ability of MOFs/ OCBs for the purpose of dye capture is shown in Fig. 8. MOFs/ OCBs can be readily recycled by a simple wash with ethanol or methanol. As shown in Fig. 8(a-1) and 8(b-1), The UV-Vis absorbance of MO decreased rapidly after different adsorption cycles, which was particular true for ZIF-8/ OCBs, nearly approaching to the baseline. Inspiringly, the regeneration efficiency of ZIF-8/ OCBs remained more than $90\%$ after four cycles and was much higher than that of HKUST-1/ OCBs $(40\%)$ , which agreed with the results of UV absorbance (shown in Fig. 8(a-2), 8(b-2)). The lower regenerated efficiency of HKUST-S/ OCBs is mainly due to the water instability of HKUST-1 crystals (Rubin et al., 2017). Besides, a slight shedding of MOFs during the handling and reuse of MOFs/ OCBs may also contribute a little to the loss of adsorption capacity and the small issue can be addressed via strengthening the affinity and bonding between the interfaces of corncobs and MOFs using some interfacial modifiers, such as carboxymethyl cellulose (CMC) and dopamine (DPA) (Zhao, Dang, Ma, & Zhu, 2019).
Although pure MOFs had better adsorption performance than MOFs/ OCBs, their poor reusability hindered its large-scale application as a dye adsorbent. Feng et al. (2016) reported that more than $60\mathrm{\wt}\%$ of ZIF-8 powders were lost after three cycles of adsorption test due to its small size. Therefore, corncobs can serve as sustainable and biodegradable carriers for high adsorption capacity of MOFs, thus endowing the as-prepared MOFs/ OCBs biosorbents with excellent recyclability without comprise of its adsorption efficiency.
## 3.9 Bacterial inactivation analyses
As shown in Fig. 9, the oxidized corncobs (OCBs) exhibited higher bacterial colonies than the original corncobs (control) (834 vs 740), due to the removal of lignin (the presence of more lignin has a negative effect on the bacterial growth) (Zhang et al., 2017). The MOFs-containing samples showed good to excellent bacterial inactivation property against E. coli, particularly, the HKUST-1 and HKUST- $1/\$ OCB samples were more effective than the ZIF-8, and ZIF-8/ OCBs samples. According to the growth inhibition of bacterial (GIB) results in Fig. 9, the bacterial inactivation property of these samples is ranked in a decreasing order of HKUST-1 $(93.4\%)$ , HKUST-1/ OCBs $(90.2\%)$ , ZIF-8 $(69.3\%)$ , ZIF-8/ OCBs $(44.8\%)$ . The disinfection mechanism may be due to the slowly sustaining release of antibacterial metal ions ( $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ and $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ) (Wang et al., 2015; Wyszogrodzka, Marszalek, Gil, & Dorozynsk, 2016) and photocatalytic induced reactive oxygen species (ROS) from the MOF materials (Li et al., 2019).
## 4. Conclusions
Corncob, an agricultural residue, was used as carrier/support for the immobilization of MOFs (HKUST-1 and ZIF-8), and the resultant composite materials (HKUST-1/ OCBs, ZIF-8/ OCBs), were studied for dyes removal and microbial inactivation from wastewater. The results showed that the as-prepared HKUST-1/ OCBs, ZIF-8/ OCBs samples have effective adsorption and bacterial inactivation performance. The MO removal efficiency increased from $8\%$ (OCBs, which is the control), to $55\%\mathrm{mg/g}$ (HKUST-1/ OCBs) and $84\%$ (ZIF-8/ OCBs) when $40\ \mathrm{mg/mL}$ of biosorbents were used and the loading of HKUST-1 and ZIF-8 onto OCBs was $86.33~\mathrm{mg/g}$ and $48.14~\mathrm{mg/g}$ , respectively. The adsorption kinetic fitted well to the pseudo-second-order model. Correspondingly, the bacterial inactivation increased from 0 (control) to $90.2\%$ (HKUST-1/ OCBs) and $44.8\%$ (ZIF-8/ OCBs). Such a concept may provide a green and sustainable strategy to use agricultural waste materials to be applied for wastewater treatment.
